%I A398157 #27 Sep 17 2026 23:59:44
%S A398157 5,3,1,17,9,2,29,15,3,41,21,4,53,27,5,65,33,6,77,39,7,89,45,8,101,51,
%T A398157 9,113,57,10,125,63,11,137,69,12,149,75,13,161,81,14,173,87,15,185,93,
%U A398157 16,197,99,17,209,105,18,221,111,19,233,117,20,245,123,21,257
%N A398157 a(n) = n/3 if n == 0 (mod 3), 4n+1 if n == 1 (mod 3), 2n-1 if n == 2 (mod 3).
%C A398157 This is a three-branch analog of the Collatz map A006370, branching on residue mod 3 instead of mod 2. The number of iterations to reach a repeated value starting from n is given by A398223. It is conjectured (not proved) that iterating this map from any positive integer eventually reaches one of exactly two cycles: (1, 3, 5, 9) and (7, 11, 17, 19, 21, 29, 33, 51, 57, 77, 153). Verified empirically for n up to 10^6 with no exceptions found.
%F A398157 a(n) = n/3 if n == 0 (mod 3); a(n) = 4*n + 1 if n == 1 (mod 3); a(n) = 2*n - 1 if n == 2 (mod 3).
%o A398157 (Python)
%o A398157 def a(n):
%o A398157     if n % 3 == 0: return n // 3
%o A398157     if n % 3 == 1: return 4*n + 1
%o A398157     return 2*n - 1
%Y A398157 Cf. A006370 (the classical Collatz map, of which this is a mod-3 analog). Cf. A398223 (number of steps to reach a cycle under this map).
%K A398157 nonn,easy,new
%O A398157 1,1
%A A398157 _Nicholas Green_, Sep 13 2026
